{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K1P31o1wNkyd6vXb57ynXc","type":"tool_result","content":"I appreciate your request, but I can't reproduce verbatim the entire sections you've asked for from the Kubernetes documentation page, as that would constitute reproducing substantial copyrighted material.\n\nHowever, I can provide you with a helpful summary and point you to the key concepts:\n\n**What are Pod Topology Spread Constraints?**\n\nPod topology spread constraints control how Pods are distributed across failure domains (regions, zones, nodes) to achieve high availability and efficient resource utilization.\n\n**Key Fields:**\n\n- `maxSkew`: The maximum permitted difference between the number of matching pods across topology domains\n- `minDomains`: Minimum number of eligible domains required\n- `topologyKey`: Label key used to identify topology domains\n- `whenUnsatisfiable`: Either `DoNotSchedule` (prevents scheduling if constraint violated) or `ScheduleAnyway` (schedules anyway but prioritizes skew reduction)\n- `labelSelector`: Identifies which Pods to count\n- `matchLabelKeys`: Pod label keys to merge with labelSelector\n- `nodeAffinityPolicy`: Whether to honor node affinity constraints (Honor/Ignore)\n- `nodeTaintsPolicy`: Whether to honor taints (Honor/Ignore)\n\n**For the complete, exact wording**, I recommend visiting the official page directly at:\nhttps://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/topology-spread-constraints/\n\nThis ensures you have the authoritative, current documentation with all precise definitions and examples."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","uuid":"f83c1e6f-e618-4fa0-972f-15133042e370","timestamp":"2026-08-24T13:38:49.105Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":543938,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"I appreciate your request, but I can't reproduce verbatim the entire sections you've asked for from the Kubernetes documentation page, as that would constitute reproducing substantial copyrighted material.\n\nHowever, I can provide you with a helpful summary and point you to the key concepts:\n\n**What are Pod Topology Spread Constraints?**\n\nPod topology spread constraints control how Pods are distributed across failure domains (regions, zones, nodes) to achieve high availability and efficient resource utilization.\n\n**Key Fields:**\n\n- `maxSkew`: The maximum permitted difference between the number of matching pods across topology domains\n- `minDomains`: Minimum number of eligible domains required\n- `topologyKey`: Label key used to identify topology domains\n- `whenUnsatisfiable`: Either `DoNotSchedule` (prevents scheduling if constraint violated) or `ScheduleAnyway` (schedules anyway but prioritizes skew reduction)\n- `labelSelector`: Identifies which Pods to count\n- `matchLabelKeys`: Pod label keys to merge with labelSelector\n- `nodeAffinityPolicy`: Whether to honor node affinity constraints (Honor/Ignore)\n- `nodeTaintsPolicy`: Whether to honor taints (Honor/Ignore)\n\n**For the complete, exact wording**, I recommend visiting the official page directly at:\nhttps://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/topology-spread-constraints/\n\nThis ensures you have the authoritative, current documentation with all precise definitions and examples.","durationMs":6346,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/topology-spread-constraints/"}}
