{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TLqVB2HySqxsp2DBPiQVrF","type":"tool_result","content":"# Pod Topology Spread Constraints - Exact Quotations\n\n1. **Opening one-sentence definition:**\n\"You can use _topology spread constraints_ to control how [Pods](/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/ \"A Pod represents a set of running containers in your cluster.\") are spread across your cluster among failure-domains such as regions, zones, nodes, and other user-defined topology domains.\"\n\n2. **Two goals (availability and resource utilization):**\n\"This can help to achieve high availability as well as efficient resource utilization.\"\n\n3. **Definition of `maxSkew`:**\n\"**maxSkew** describes the degree to which Pods may be unevenly distributed. You must specify this field and the number must be greater than zero.\"\n\n4. **Definition of \"skew\" and formula:**\nNOT PRESENT ON PAGE. (The page uses the term \"skew\" extensively but does not provide an explicit definition sentence or mathematical formula for it. It describes how `maxSkew` semantics differ based on `whenUnsatisfiable` values, but does not define \"skew\" itself as a standalone concept with a formula.)\n\n5. **Definition of `topologyKey`:**\n\"**topologyKey** is the key of [node labels](#node-labels). Nodes that have a label with this key and identical values are considered to be in the same topology.\"\n\n6. **Definition of `whenUnsatisfiable` with exact meanings:**\n\"**whenUnsatisfiable** indicates how to deal with a Pod if it doesn't satisfy the spread constraint:\n\n    *   `DoNotSchedule` (default) tells the scheduler not to schedule it.\n    *   `ScheduleAnyway` tells the scheduler to still schedule it while prioritizing nodes that minimize the skew.\"\n\n7. **Definitions of `labelSelector` and `minDomains`:**\n\n   - **labelSelector:** \"**labelSelector** is used to find matching Pods. Pods that match this label selector are counted to determine the number of Pods in their corresponding topology domain.\"\n   \n   - **minDomains:** \"**minDomains** indicates a minimum number of eligible domains. This field is optional. A domain is a particular instance of a topology. An eligible domain is a domain whose nodes match the node selector.\"\n\n8. **Relationship to pod affinity/anti-affinity:**\n\"## Comparison with podAffinity and podAntiAffinity\" (section header present, but the quoted text is truncated due to content length limits, so the actual comparison content is not visible in the provided excerpt.)\n\n9. **Mention of cluster-level default constraints:**\n\"You can set [cluster-level constraints](#cluster-level-default-constraints) as a default, or configure topology spread constraints for individual workloads.\"\n\n10. **Stated limitations or caveats:**\nNOT PRESENT ON PAGE (in the provided excerpt). The page has a \"Known limitations\" section mentioned in the table of contents, but the actual content of that section is not included in the truncated text provided."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","uuid":"8ec90f9a-07ae-4342-998a-330c40cd6f09","timestamp":"2026-08-24T13:39:28.221Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":543938,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Pod Topology Spread Constraints - Exact Quotations\n\n1. **Opening one-sentence definition:**\n\"You can use _topology spread constraints_ to control how [Pods](/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/ \"A Pod represents a set of running containers in your cluster.\") are spread across your cluster among failure-domains such as regions, zones, nodes, and other user-defined topology domains.\"\n\n2. **Two goals (availability and resource utilization):**\n\"This can help to achieve high availability as well as efficient resource utilization.\"\n\n3. **Definition of `maxSkew`:**\n\"**maxSkew** describes the degree to which Pods may be unevenly distributed. You must specify this field and the number must be greater than zero.\"\n\n4. **Defin