{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J99kXrpsAw5AmEXYgM1Vyn","type":"tool_result","content":"# Kubernetes Taints and Tolerations - Key Quotes\n\n## 1. Taint based Evictions Opening\n\n\"The node controller automatically taints a Node when certain conditions are true.\"\n\n\"In some cases when the node is unreachable, the API server is unable to communicate with the kubelet on the node.\"\n\n## 2. Taint Nodes by Condition Section\n\n\"The control plane, using the node controller, automatically creates taints with a `NoSchedule` effect for node conditions. The scheduler checks taints, not node conditions, when it makes scheduling decisions. This ensures that node conditions don't directly affect scheduling.\"\n\nThe mappings: `node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure` (DiskPressure), `node.kubernetes.io/memory-pressure` (MemoryPressure).\n\n## 3. tolerationSeconds Behaviour\n\n\"Pods that tolerate the taint with a specified `tolerationSeconds` remain bound for the specified amount of time. After that time elapses, the node lifecycle controller evicts the Pods.\"\n\n## 4. Exists Operator with No Key\n\n\"If the `key` is empty, then the `operator` must be `Exists`, which matches all keys and values.\"\n\n## 5. Multiple Taints Filtering\n\n\"start with all of a node's taints, then ignore the ones for which the pod has a matching toleration; the remaining un-ignored taints have the indicated effects on the pod.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","uuid":"085c144a-8073-4938-82bb-1789fe6bcb84","timestamp":"2026-08-24T13:40:33.011Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":18854,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Kubernetes Taints and Tolerations - Key Quotes\n\n## 1. Taint based Evictions Opening\n\n\"The node controller automatically taints a Node when certain conditions are true.\"\n\n\"In some cases when the node is unreachable, the API server is unable to communicate with the kubelet on the node.\"\n\n## 2. Taint Nodes by Condition Section\n\n\"The control plane, using the node controller, automatically creates taints with a `NoSchedule` effect for node conditions. The scheduler checks taints, not node conditions, when it makes scheduling decisions. This ensures that node conditions don't directly affect scheduling.\"\n\nThe mappings: `node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure` (DiskPressure), `node.kubernetes.io/memory-pressure` (MemoryPressure).\n\n## 3. tolerationSeconds Behaviour\n\n\"Pods that tolerate the taint with a specified `tolerationSeconds` remain bound for the specified amount of time. After that time elapses, the node lifecycle controller evicts the Pods.\"\n\n## 4. Exists Operator with No Key\n\n\"If the `key` is empty, then the `operator` must be `Exists`, which matches all keys and values.\"\n\n## 5. Multiple Taints Filtering\n\n\"start with all of a node's taints, then ignore the ones for which the pod has a matching toleration; the remaining un-ignored taints have the indicated effects on the pod.\"","durationMs":4576,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/taint-and-toleration.md"}}
