{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" constraints are\n\n**[VERBATIM]**\n\n> You can use _topology spread constraints_ to control how Pods are spread across your\n> cluster among failure-domains such as regions, zones, nodes, and other user-defined\n> topology dom"}},"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"4f16fc6d-694d-4c34-b54e-f2d1c07e56c5"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ains.\n\n**[VERBATIM]**\n\n> This can help to achieve high availability as well as efficient resource utilization.\n\n**[VERBATIM]**\n\n> You can set cluster-level constraints as a default, or configure topology spread\n> constraints for individual work"}},"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"7b6d28e7-79ef-4814-a66a-7c99e7c3235c"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"loads.\n\n## Motivation\n\n**[VERBATIM]**\n\n> Imagine that you have a cluster of up to twenty nodes, and you want to run a workload\n> that automatically scales how many replicas it uses. There could be as few as two Pods\n> or as many as"}},"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"e4a8a741-835d-4b68-8ba8-bb05ca9cdc46"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" fifteen. When there are only two Pods, you'd prefer not to have both of\n> those Pods run on the same node: you would run the risk that a single node failure\n> takes your workload offline.\n>\n> In addition to this"}},"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"7027b508-8a83-4418-a08f-c426ca7fc76e"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" basic usage, there are some advanced usage examples that enable\n> your workloads to benefit on high availability and cluster utilization.\n>\n> As you scale up and run more Pods, a different concern becomes important. Im"}},"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"0851b426-6d9b-43a8-b498-58dd9c7ab05c"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"agine that\n> you have three nodes running five Pods each. The nodes have enough capacity to run that\n> many replicas; however, the clients that interact with this workload are split across\n> three different datacenters (or inf"}},"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"6ed79372-1f9d-4d92-9ad9-d194da15be01"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"rastructure zones). Now you have less concern about\n> a single node failure, but you notice that latency is higher than you'd like, and you\n> are paying for network costs associated with sending network traffic between the\n> different zones.\n>\n> You"}},"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"28256253-8ca1-47da-95f0-2a90ff033c5d"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" decide that under normal operation you'd prefer to have a similar number of\n> replicas scheduled into each infrastructure zone, and you'd like the cluster to\n> self-heal in the case that there is a problem.\n>\n> Pod topology spread constraints"}},"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"9f7e3373-026f-4765-a003-21c0533d86a0"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" offer you a declarative way to configure that.\n\n## Node labels and topology\n\n**[VERBATIM]**\n\n> Topology spread constraints rely on node labels to identify the topology domain(s) that\n> each node is in.\n\n**[PART"}},"session_id":"c5de4450-c1f8-40eb-9c44-c8ddc092eae4","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"a414e595-d166-41b0-8d87-2741a1ebecaa"}
